{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015vBydpccrngCjjS1aTJuVw","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /private/tmp/h-G1mPAG/README.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b831005c-a444-4830-bc83-e9ddc7a71969","uuid":"f46cfff0-d55e-4a27-a6c4-dd1767ba403a","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:13:19.799Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/h-G1mPAG/README.md","oldString":"Prompt caching is on: the system\nprompt carries a `cache_control` marker so the tool-loop rounds and follow-up\nmessages inside the 5-minute window read it from cache.","newString":"Prompt caching is on: the system\nprompt is two blocks, each with a `cache_control` marker. The static instructions\ncome first so a `save_fact` write (which changes only the per-athlete block) does\nnot evict them. Tool-loop rounds and follow-up messages inside the 5-minute window\nread from cache.","originalFile":"# pace-bot\n\nA coaching chat bot on Claude for a small running/cycling club. It is stateless\nby design: each message rebuilds the system prompt (coaching rules + the athlete's\nprofile, zones, goal and long-term facts from `data/athletes.json`), picks the tools\nthe message might need (`save_fact` and `log_session` always, planning or race tools\nwhen keywords match) and runs a short tool-use loop. `save_fact` is how the bot\nremembers durable things an athlete tells it; the next message sees the fact in\nits system prompt. Every API call appends its `usage` object to `logs/usage.jsonl`.\n\nRun it locally with `COACH_ANTHROPIC_KEY=sk-ant-... python3 bot.py 42` and type\nmessages on stdin (the Telegram long-polling transport, which needs\n`COACH_TELEGRAM_TOKEN`, lives in a separate deploy repo and just calls `reply()`).\nRequires Python 3.11+ and `pip install anthropic`. Prompt caching is on: the system\nprompt carries a `cache_control` marker so the tool-loop rounds and follow-up\nmessages inside the 5-minute window read it from cache.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":12,"oldLines":5,"newStart":12,"newLines":7,"lines":[" messages on stdin (the Telegram long-polling transport, which needs"," `COACH_TELEGRAM_TOKEN`, lives in a separate deploy repo and just calls `reply()`)."," Requires Python 3.11+ and `pip install anthropic`. Prompt caching is on: the system","-prompt carries a `cache_control` marker so the tool-loop rounds and follow-up","-messages inside the 5-minute window read it from cache.","+prompt is two blocks, each with a `cache_control` marker. The static instructions","+come first so a `save_fact` write (which changes only the per-athlete block) does","+not evict them. Tool-loop rounds and follow-up messages inside the 5-minute window","+read from cache."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015vBydpccrngCjjS1aTJuVw","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS6ECdd135VCwVnpcZAc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RBFBNzR6k7sERZbJhNe5uz","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-G1mPAG/ANSWER.md","content":"# Fix: memory writes evicting the static system prompt\n\n## Cause\n\n`build_system()` in `bot.py` returned one text block: `INSTRUCTIONS` plus the\nper-athlete context (date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language), with a single\n`cache_control` marker at the end. Caching is a prefix hash, so every `save_fact`\nchanged the facts inside that block, changed the hash, and evicted the whole\nprompt, including the instructions that never change.\n\n`logs/usage.jsonl` shows it. The call after a `save_fact` has\n`cache_read_input_tokens: 0` and `cache_creation_input_tokens` around the full\nprompt size (about 1050 to 1100 tokens), for example the `save_fact` rounds at\n2026-04-18 06:43:16 and 08:02:33.\n\n## Change\n\n`bot.py`, `build_system()`: the system prompt is now two text blocks, each with\n`cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`:\n\n1. `INSTRUCTIONS`, a constant. Byte-identical across athletes, days and memory writes.\n2. `athlete_context(a, today)`, the volatile tail.\n\nA `save_fact` write now invalidates only block 2. Block 1 should still be read\nfrom cache. Checked offline: block 1 is identical for two athletes on different\ndays and after a fact is added, and block 2 changes.\n\n`README.md`: the caching paragraph described the single marker. It now describes\nthe two-block layout.\n\nNothing else changed. Tool selection, the save path and the tool loop are as before.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n- **Applied: two `cache_control` markers, static instructions first, per-user tail second.**\n  This is the same fix, and the `build_system` comment records the rule that\n  `INSTRUCTIONS` must stay free of interpolated values.\n- **Applied: the check to run.** Send two messages within 5 minutes, then one that\n  triggers `save_fact`, then one more. The last call should show `cache_read` covering\n  the static block and `cache_creation` near the tail size only. If `cache_creation`\n  is the full prompt size again, something in block 1 is not byte-stable.\n- **Applied, with a caveat: keep the tools array stable.** `save_fact` and `log_session`\n  are always sent, so memory writes don't change the tools. The keyword groups\n  (`plan_week`, `predict_race`) do change the tools array, which sits above the system\n  prompt in the cache prefix. The usage log shows this: the `plan_week` call at\n  06:44:31 has `cache_read_input_tokens: 0`. I did not change this. Fixing it means\n  either sending all tools every time, which gives up the keyword-filter saving, or\n  accepting the occasional re-cache. The colleague's note makes the same trade-off.\n- **Not applied: top-N fact selection.** This bot already loads all facts every time,\n  so there was nothing to change.\n- **Not applied: the 780 / 240 token figures and the Telegram setup.** Those are their\n  numbers for their bot. The Telegram transport is not in this repo.\n\n## Open risk: the minimum cacheable length\n\nThe first block may be below the minimum cacheable prompt length. To my knowledge\nthat minimum is 1024 tokens for Sonnet-class models, so please check it against the\ndocs. The static prefix is `INSTRUCTIONS` (about 2,480 characters, roughly 620 tokens by\na chars/4 estimate) plus the core tool schemas (about 420 characters of JSON). That is\nprobably around 750 to 900 tokens, under 1024. If so, the marker on block 1 is ignored\nsilently, and a memory write will still re-cache everything: nothing breaks, but the\nfix does nothing.\n\nI could not check this. There is no API key in the sandbox, and I have not called the\nAPI. The check that settles it is `messages.count_tokens` on the tools plus the static\nblock, or the first real `save_fact` after deploy. If `cache_creation` after the write\nis about the tail size, the fix works. If it is about the full prompt again and the\nstatic block is under the minimum, the options are: make the instructions substantially\nlonger with real content (a product decision, not a padding trick), or accept the\nre-cache.\n\n## Verification status\n\n- Offline check: passed, as described above.\n- Live API check: not done. Needs a key and the steps under \"Open risk\".\n- Not committed. The working tree has `bot.py` and `README.md` modified, plus this file.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":511,"cache_read_input_tokens":37833,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":511},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b831005c-a444-4830-bc83-e9ddc7a71969","uuid":"ad25c6c8-25db-489a-a934-dbf1c18f6999","timestamp":"2026-10-27T04:13:27.102Z","request_id":"req_011CgS6ECMzhR9U6yAWY9qrK","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01RBFBNzR6k7sERZbJhNe5uz":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-G1mPAG/ANSWER.md","content":"# Fix: memory writes evicting the static system prompt\n\n## Cause\n\n`build_system()` in `bot.py` returned one text block: `INSTRUCTIONS` plus the\nper-athlete context (date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language), with a single\n`cache_control` marker at the end. Caching is a prefix hash, so every `save_fact`\nchanged the facts inside that block, changed the hash, and evicted the whole\nprompt, including the instructions that never change.\n\n`logs/usage.jsonl` shows it. The call after a `save_fact` has\n`cache_read_input_tokens: 0` and `cache_creation_input_tokens` around the full\nprompt size (about 1050 to 1100 tokens), for example the `save_fact` rounds at\n2026-04-18 06:43:16 and 08:02:33.\n\n## Change\n\n`bot.py`, `build_system()`: the system prompt is now two text blocks, each with\n`cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`:\n\n1. `INSTRUCTIONS`, a constant. Byte-identical across athletes, days and memory writes.\n2. `athlete_context(a, today)`, the volatile tail.\n\nA `save_fact` write now invalidates only block 2. Block 1 should still be read\nfrom cache. Checked offline: block 1 is identical for two athletes on different\ndays and after a fact is added, and block 2 changes.\n\n`README.md`: the caching paragraph described the single marker. It now describes\nthe two-block layout.\n\nNothing else changed. Tool selection, the save path and the tool loop are as before.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n- **Applied: two `cache_control` markers, static instructions first, per-user tail second.**\n  This is the same fix, and the `build_system` comment records the rule that\n  `INSTRUCTIONS` must stay free of interpolated values.\n- **Applied: the check to run.** Send two messages within 5 minutes, then one that\n  triggers `save_fact`, then one more. The last call should show `cache_read` covering\n  the static block and `cache_creation` near the tail size only. If `cache_creation`\n  is the full prompt size again, something in block 1 is not byte-stable.\n- **Applied, with a caveat: keep the tools array stable.** `save_fact` and `log_session`\n  are always sent, so memory writes don't change the tools. The keyword groups\n  (`plan_week`, `predict_race`) do change the tools array, which sits above the system\n  prompt in the cache prefix. The usage log shows this: the `plan_week` call at\n  06:44:31 has `cache_read_input_tokens: 0`. I did not change this. Fixing it means\n  either sending all tools every time, which gives up the keyword-filter saving, or\n  accepting the occasional re-cache. The colleague's note makes the same trade-off.\n- **Not applied: top-N fact selection.** This bot already loads all facts every time,\n  so there was nothing to change.\n- **Not applied: the 780 / 240 token figures and the Telegram setup.** Those are their\n  numbers for their bot. The Telegram transport is not in this repo.\n\n## Open risk: the minimum cacheable length\n\nThe first block may be below the minimum cacheable prompt length. To my knowledge\nthat minimum is 1024 tokens for Sonnet-class models, so please check it against the\ndocs. The static prefix is `INSTRUCTIONS` (about 2,480 characters, roughly 620 tokens by\na chars/4 estimate) plus the core tool schemas (about 420 characters of JSON). That is\nprobably around 750 to 900 tokens, under 1024. If so, the marker on block 1 is ignored\nsilently, and a memory write will still re-cache everything: nothing breaks, but the\nfix does nothing.\n\nI could not check this. There is no API key in the sandbox, and I have not called the\nAPI. The check that settles it is `messages.count_tokens` on the tools plus the static\nblock, or the first real `save_fact` after deploy. If `cache_creation` after the write\nis about the tail size, the fix works. If it is about the full prompt again and the\nstatic block is under the minimum, the options are: make the instructions substantially\nlonger with real content (a product decision, not a padding trick), or accept the\nre-cache.\n\n## Verification status\n\n- Offline check: passed, as described above.\n- Live API check: not done. Needs a key and the steps under \"Open risk\".\n- Not committed. The working tree has `bot.py` and `README.md` modified, plus this file.\n"}}}
